# pubscan.local -- author-specific identifiers, NEVER committed.
#
# Copy to `ci/pubscan.local` and fill in your own. .gitignore excludes that
# name, which is the entire point: a published scanner that carries the literal
# string it is looking for has disclosed it. This file previously held a real
# hostname and two personal domains as regexes, in a public repo.
#
# ⚠ WITHOUT ci/pubscan.local, PUBSCAN CANNOT GIVE A CLEAN RESULT, and it says
# so rather than implying one. A fresh clone has no local file, so the
# author-specific detectors do not exist -- and on 2026-09-07 those were the
# ONLY detectors that caught anything: an author hostname in pubscan.py's own
# docstring, a monitoring host's DNS name, and author paths in a sibling repo.
# The structural detectors that survive a clone would have found none of them.
# Restore this file before treating any repo as checked.
#
# Format: label<TAB>regex   (one per line, matched case-insensitively)
# Lines that are blank, start with #, or contain no tab are ignored.
#
# Anything structural -- private address ranges, MAC shapes, key headers,
# Windows user paths, EMAIL ADDRESS SHAPES -- belongs in pubscan.py itself,
# where it identifies nobody and everyone benefits from it. Only put a literal
# here.
#
# `personal email` used to live in this file, and that was a real hole: a fresh
# clone had NO email detector at all and still printed a clean summary. A
# detector that cannot fire reads exactly like one that found nothing. It is now
# structural, in pubscan.py, with selftest cases. Do not add it back here.

author hostnames	\b(my-workstation|my-forge-host)\b
author domains	\b[a-z0-9-]+\.(?:example|example-two)\.com\b
author paths	[A-Za-z]:\\{1,2}(?:MyRepoDir|MyProjectsDir)\b

# ACCOUNT NAMES, and scope them to login position rather than matching bare.
# A tool shipped `--user default="MyName"` into a commit staged for a public
# push and every structural detector passed it: an account name is not an
# address, a MAC, a path, a hostname or an email. On a device it is half a
# credential.
#
# Bare `\bMyName\b` matched 69 times across 26 files here -- prose, docs,
# quoted instructions -- and the name is public anyway through git authorship.
# Drowning the one hit that matters is the failure the placeholder-MAC filter
# exists to prevent. Scope it, then check it still fires on the real case:
# the first scoped attempt was too tight and matched nothing at all, which
# looked exactly like being clean.
author accounts	(?i)\b(user|username|account|login|acct)\b[^\n]{0,24}["']MyName["']
